					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1775542&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200941" alt="augmentedspartan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1775542)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;" >augmentedspartan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1565906542 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 Aug 2019 22:02</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4331676">
									

<p>I'm not a fan of the purple prose, myself. Not the concept of using it on an SCP (as long as it makes sense in context as it does here) but rather the writing itself didn't reach me. Nothing in particular that I can point out, it's just that the imagery never really did much for me.</p>
<p>My biggest problem with the article is the type of hell presented. Eternally repeating the worst moment of your life is a concept that I've grown bored of, doesn't have much entertaining imagery, and is just straight up not scary compared to the concept of eternal torture. To me it's a very weak payoff to all the buildup presented, especially because the moment described doesn't really strike close to me.</p>
<p>Personally I would have been more interested if you would have expanded on the concept of cosmic punishment itself, maybe explore the why or the concept of a universal morality, but just throwing a very subjective hell doesn't really drive forth that theme you were talking about.</p>

							</div>

								
					
